---
import HelpLayout from '../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join } from 'node:path';

/**
 * The `.badger` file format (M-web-4, spec: badger-fit
 * docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-m-web-4-orphaned-pages.md section 2).
 *
 * Source of truth is the app's share_payload.dart (the shape) and
 * import_service.dart (the rules). Every field table below was derived from
 * those two files, not from memory. When either changes, update this page and
 * the "last verified" line at the bottom.
 *
 * The page has two parts: a short one for people, then the specification,
 * ordered envelope, routine, day, exercise and slot, set, progression rule,
 * equipment. Compatibility notes live in one section at the end; only the
 * "Needs Badger 1.1.12 or later" markers stay inline.
 *
 * The worked example is not typed out here: it is read from the real
 * public/programs/example-routine.badger at build time and pretty-printed, so
 * the example and the downloadable file can never disagree.
 */

// Resolved from the project root, not from import.meta.url: at build time this
// module has been bundled into dist/, so a module-relative path points at a
// directory that does not exist. A wrong root fails the build loudly, which is
// the behavior we want for a file the page cannot render without.
const exampleRaw = readFileSync(
  join(process.cwd(), 'public/programs/example-routine.badger'),
  'utf8',
);
const example = JSON.parse(exampleRaw);
const examplePretty = JSON.stringify(example, null, 2);
const appVersion = example.app_version;

// The progression example is built only from keys and values this page
// defines, with the clause order the shipped StrongLifts file uses. Written as
// an object so it is always valid JSON.
const ruleExample = {
  exercise_name: 'Barbell Back Squat',
  exercise_type: 'weight_reps',
  progression: {
    scheme: 'linear',
    cadence: 'session',
    reference_kind: 'working_weight',
    clauses: [
      {
        trigger: 'consecutive_failures_at_least',
        count: 3,
        action: 'add_percent',
        target: 'working_weight',
        percent: -10,
      },
      {
        trigger: 'all_sets_met',
        action: 'add_absolute',
        target: 'working_weight',
        amount: 2.5,
        amount_unit: 'kg',
      },
      { trigger: 'always', action: 'hold', target: 'working_weight' },
    ],
  },
  predefined_sets: [
    { is_warmup: false, reps: 5 },
    { is_warmup: false, reps: 5 },
    { is_warmup: false, reps: 5 },
  ],
};
const ruleExamplePretty = JSON.stringify(ruleExample, null, 2);

const description =
  'How a .badger file is put together: the share/v1 envelope, the routine, gym and machine payloads, what import does with each field, and a worked example.';
---

<HelpLayout title="The .badger file format" description={description}>
  <h1>The <code>.badger</code> file format</h1>

  <p>
    A <code>.badger</code> file is how Badger hands a routine, a gym, or a set of machines to someone
    else. It is a single JSON text file, so you can open one in any text editor and read all of it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-holds">What a file holds</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A routine file</strong> holds a program: its days, exercises, planned sets and rep
      targets, supersets and tempo, and, where the program has them, progression rules, percentage
      sets and weekly waves.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A gym file</strong> holds a gym's equipment: machines, attachments, bars, accessories,
      dumbbell and kettlebell racks, and plates.
    </li>
    <li><strong>A machines file</strong> holds just machines.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A file never holds your workout history: no logged workouts, sets or PRs, no training max, and
    nothing about you. To move your own history to a new phone, use a backup instead (see
    <a href="/help/phone-migration/">moving to a new phone</a>). The full list is under
    <a href="#not-carried">what a file does not carry</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="opening">Opening a file on your phone</h2>

  <p>
    Tap the file wherever it arrived (a message, an email, a download), or open it from your file
    manager (Files on an iPhone). Badger shows a preview of what the file will add before anything
    is saved.
  </p>

  <p>
    Importing only adds. Nothing you already have is changed, and exercises you already have are
    matched by name and type, so they keep their history.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ready-made">Where to find ready-made files</h2>

  <p>
    <a href="/programs/">Programs</a> has every program built into Badger as a file you can download
    and open. To have an AI write one for you, the <a href="/guides/ai/">Use your own AI</a> guide
    has a prompt for it. The rest of this page is the specification, for anyone writing a file by
    hand, with a tool, or with an AI.
  </p>

  <h2 id="specification">Specification</h2>

  <p>
    From here to the end, this page defines what a file may contain and what import does with it.
    Fields marked <em>required</em> must be present. Any other field may be left out or written as
    <code>null</code>, and either reads as absent. Unknown fields are ignored.
  </p>

  <p>
    Keys marked "Needs Badger 1.1.12 or later" are ignored by older versions; see
    <a href="#versioning">versions and compatibility</a>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="clause-terms">Terms used below</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Slot</strong>: one exercise on one day of a routine, with its planned sets. A slot is
      a <a href="#day-exercise"><code>DayExercise</code></a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Progression group</strong>: the slots of one routine that share a
      <a href="#day-exercise"><code>progression_group_key</code></a>, on any of its days. They
      progress as one: a single reference weight, failure counter, stage and remembered weight
      between them. A rule in the group reads sessions from its own day and from the days of the
      other members that carry a rule. A slot with no key is a group of its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Training max</strong>: the number a set's percentage is taken from. A progression
      rule moves it, and it never goes on the bar itself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reference weight</strong>: the number a slot's percentage sets are a percentage of.
      Under a rule whose <code>reference_kind</code> is <code>training_max</code>, it is the training
      max. Each lifter has their own, and a file never carries it. Slots in one progression group
      share one. A slot with no reference weight of its own takes another slot's; see
      <a href="#day-exercise"><code>percent_reference</code></a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Waved day</strong>: a day whose sets change by week of a repeating cycle (5/3/1's four
      weeks) or by stage. Each version of the day's sets is a <strong>variant</strong>. See
      <a href="#waves">Waves</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stage</strong>: on a stage-based day (GZCLP, StrongLifts), which of the day's set
      variants the slot is on, counting from 0. Only <code>next_stage</code> and
      <code>reset_stage</code> move it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Driver set</strong>: the one planned set whose logged reps some triggers read, marked
      with <a href="#predefined-set"><code>is_progression_driver</code></a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Missed session</strong> (a failure): a session of this slot in which at least one
      logged working set was left incomplete or came in under its planned minimum. A skipped set
      counts neither way. A session where every logged working set met its minimum is a success. A
      session with no completed set of the exercise at all is neither, and the rule ignores it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Failure counter</strong>: the run of missed sessions in a row. A success resets it to
      0, and so does a <code>consecutive_failures_at_least</code> or <code>stage_is_last</code>
      clause firing. <code>hold</code> lets it move; <code>none</code> undoes this session's change
      to it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Remembered weight</strong>: the slot's weight as it stood while the slot was on its
      first stage, recorded each time the rule reads a session there.
      <code>set_from_remembered</code> restarts from it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="envelope">The envelope</h2>

  <p>Every file has the same five top-level fields. Import needs three of them.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>badger</code></td>
          <td>string, required</td>
          <td>
            Schema version. Today it is always <code>share/v1</code>. A file with any other value is
            refused.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>app_version</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>The app version that wrote the file, for example <code>1.1.1</code>. Informational.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>created_at</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>UTC timestamp in ISO 8601. Informational.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>type</code></td>
          <td>string, required</td>
          <td>
            One of <code>routine</code>, <code>gym</code>, <code>machines</code>. Any other value and
            the file is rejected as invalid.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>payload</code></td>
          <td>object, required</td>
          <td>The contents. Its shape depends on <code>type</code>: a <a href="#routine">routine payload</a> or a <a href="#gym">gym or machines payload</a>.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Files Badger writes leave optional fields out rather than writing <code>null</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="routine">A routine payload</h2>

  <p>
    <code>type: "routine"</code>. The payload has four keys. Only <code>routine</code> is required:
    a missing <code>days</code>, <code>exercises</code> or <code>machines</code> reads as an empty
    list, though a routine needs days and exercise definitions to be of any use.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Key</th><th>Type</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>routine</code></td><td><a href="#routine-object"><code>Routine</code></a>, required</td><td>The routine itself: name, notes, and its settings.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>days</code></td><td>array of <a href="#day"><code>Day</code></a></td><td>The days, in order. Each day carries its own <code>exercises</code> array: that day's slots, in order, with their planned sets.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>exercises</code></td><td>array of <a href="#exercise"><code>Exercise</code></a></td><td>The definition of every exercise any day references: its name, type, and category. Slots point into this list by <code>exercise_name</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>machines</code></td><td>array of <a href="#machine"><code>Machine</code></a></td><td>Machines the slots pin. May be left out. The import preview asks before creating any.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Two arrays are called <code>exercises</code>. The top-level one is an array of
    <a href="#exercise"><code>Exercise</code></a> and defines what each exercise is. Each day's
    <code>exercises</code> is an array of <a href="#day-exercise"><code>DayExercise</code></a>: that
    day's slots, which point at the definitions by name. A slot whose name matches no definition is
    skipped, and the import preview warns about it.
  </p>

  <h3 id="routine-object">Routine</h3>

  <p>The object in the payload's <code>routine</code> key.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>string, required</td><td>The routine name.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>notes</code></td><td>string</td><td>Program-level notes.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>weight_source</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            Where starting weights come from: <code>last_performance</code> (default),
            <code>routine_defaults</code>, or <code>routine_defaults_then_last</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>history_scope</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            Which history the weight lookup reads: <code>any_workout</code> (default) or
            <code>this_routine</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="day">Day</h3>

  <p>The objects in <code>days</code>, one per session in the program.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>string, required</td><td>Day name, for example "Push" or "Workout A".</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>scheduled_days</code></td>
          <td>array of integers</td>
          <td>
            Weekdays this day is scheduled on: <code>[1, 3, 5]</code> means Monday, Wednesday,
            Friday. 1 is always Monday and 7 is always Sunday, whatever the app's first-day-of-week
            setting and wherever the file is opened. Values outside 1 to 7 are dropped on import.
            Leave the field out for a program you rotate through instead of pinning to weekdays.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>default_gym_name</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>Gym this day defaults to. The gym is created on import if the name is new.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>cycle_length</code></td>
          <td>integer</td>
          <td>
            How many set variants this day cycles through, if it is <a href="#waves">waved</a>:
            <code>4</code> for a 5/3/1 day (four weeks), <code>3</code> for a GZCLP day (three
            stages). Leave it out for a day that runs the same session every time. When it is
            present, each set's <a href="#predefined-set"><code>week_index</code></a> says which
            variant the set belongs to. <a href="#waves">Waves</a> explains what picks the variant.
            Needs Badger 1.1.12 or later.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><code>exercises</code></td><td>array of <a href="#day-exercise"><code>DayExercise</code></a></td><td>The day's slots. Left out, the day imports empty.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="exercise">Exercise</h3>

  <p>
    The objects in the payload's top-level <code>exercises</code>: the definitions the slots point
    at. List only the exercises the routine uses. Import matches each one against the recipient's
    library first, so most usually resolve to exercises they already have.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>name</code></td>
          <td>string, required</td>
          <td>
            Matching is by name, so plain full names travel best. "Barbell Back Squat" lands on the
            recipient's existing squat; "BB Squat (heavy day)" creates a new exercise.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>type</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            One of <code>weight_reps</code> (the default), <code>reps_only</code>,
            <code>time_only</code>, <code>distance_only</code>, <code>weight_only</code>,
            <code>weight_time</code>, <code>weight_distance</code>, <code>reps_time</code>,
            <code>reps_distance</code>, <code>distance_time</code>. An unrecognized value falls back to
            <code>weight_reps</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>category_name</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            Category for an exercise that has to be created. Any name is valid: an existing category
            is reused by name, a new name creates one, and absent reads as "Other". A fresh install
            has Chest, Back, Shoulders, Biceps, Triceps, Legs, Core, and Cardio. These are not a fixed
            list, since categories can be renamed or deleted, but using them puts new exercises where
            most libraries already sort them.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="day-exercise">DayExercise</h3>

  <p>
    The objects in a <a href="#day"><code>Day</code></a>'s <code>exercises</code> array. Each is one
    slot: one exercise on that day, with its planned sets.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>exercise_name</code></td>
          <td>string, required</td>
          <td>Must match a <code>name</code> in the payload's <code>exercises</code> list.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>exercise_type</code></td>
          <td>string, same values as <a href="#exercise"><code>Exercise</code></a>.<code>type</code></td>
          <td>
            The referenced exercise's type, repeated here. Optional, but include it: it tells a
            "Plank" you time apart from a "Plank" someone counts reps on. When present it must
            exactly equal the definition's <code>type</code>, because import matches the slot by
            name and type together. A mismatch matches nothing, and the slot is skipped and counted
            in the import preview's warning, like an unmatched name.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>rest_override_seconds</code></td>
          <td>integer</td>
          <td>Rest for this slot, when it should differ from the exercise's own default.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>default_machine_name</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            Machine this slot is pinned to, by name. Import looks for it among the recipient's own
            machines and the payload's <code>machines</code> list, so a machine the recipient already
            has does not need to be in the file. Include it in <code>machines</code> if the file
            should also work for someone who lacks it. A name that matches nothing leaves the slot
            unpinned. Import links the matched machine to the exercise.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>superset_key</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            Slots in the same day sharing a key become a superset, in the order they appear. The key
            can be anything (<code>"1"</code>, <code>"a"</code>); it only has to match between the
            members. A key held by a single slot is ignored.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>superset_name</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            Display name for the superset. The first member's value wins. Leave it out and the app
            names the group itself. Group color does not travel: the recipient's own palette assigns
            it.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>tempo</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>Tempo prescription for the slot, carried word for word, for example <code>"3-1-2-0"</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>alternatives</code></td>
          <td>array of objects</td>
          <td>
            Swap options for this slot, in order. Each is
            <code>&#123; "exercise_name": "...", "exercise_type": "..." &#125;</code>, with
            <code>exercise_name</code> required, and is matched by the same rules as the slot itself,
            so each needs a definition in the payload's <code>exercises</code> list. An unmatched
            alternative is skipped and counted in the import preview's warning.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>progression</code></td>
          <td><a href="#progression"><code>Progression</code></a></td>
          <td>
            This slot's progression rule: what happens to its weight next session. At most one per
            slot. Leave the key out for a slot with no rule. Needs Badger 1.1.12 or later.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>progression_group_key</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            Slots anywhere in the routine sharing a key form one
            <a href="#clause-terms">progression group</a> and progress as one. StrongLifts groups
            the squat on Workout A with the squat on Workout B, so three misses in a row count across
            both days. Only the sessions of members that carry a <code>progression</code> rule move
            the group; a member with no rule reads the group's number, as Madcow's Monday and
            Wednesday squats do. Give different lifts, and two tiers of one lift (GZCLP's T1 and T2),
            different keys.
            <br /><br />
            The key can be anything (<code>"sl-squat"</code>, <code>"1"</code>); it only has to match
            between the members, case included. Surrounding spaces are ignored, and an empty key
            reads as absent. Import gives each group a new key of its own and keeps who is in it.
            Leave the key out and the slot's progress is its own; a key held by a single slot works
            the same way. Needs Badger 1.1.12 or later.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>percent_reference</code></td>
          <td>object</td>
          <td>
            The lift this slot's <a href="#predefined-set"><code>percent_of_reference</code></a>
            sets are a percentage of, when it is a different lift: close-grip bench at 80% of the
            bench press's training max. Written like an alternative,
            <code>&#123; "exercise_name": "...", "exercise_type": "..." &#125;</code> with
            <code>exercise_name</code> required, and matched against the payload's
            <code>exercises</code> list by the same rules.
            <br /><br />
            It applies while the slot has no reference weight of its own. The slot then takes the
            reference weight of a slot in this routine that programs the named lift and carries a
            rule, and looks nowhere else. Leave the key out and the reference weight comes from the
            slot's progression group, then from a ruled slot in the routine programming the same
            exercise.
            <br /><br />
            A name that matches no definition, or that names the slot's own exercise, reads as
            absent. A lift the routine does not program is kept: the log preview says those sets
            need a reference weight, and their weight can be typed in. Do not put the two lifts in
            one progression group to get this, since a group also shares one failure counter and
            one stage. Needs Badger 1.1.12 or later.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>predefined_sets</code></td>
          <td>array of <a href="#predefined-set"><code>PredefinedSet</code></a></td>
          <td>The planned sets. May be left out or empty.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="predefined-set">PredefinedSet</h3>

  <p>
    The objects in a <a href="#day-exercise"><code>DayExercise</code></a>'s
    <code>predefined_sets</code>, one per planned set. Fill in the fields the exercise type uses:
    weight and reps for a bench press, <code>duration_seconds</code> for a plank,
    <code>distance</code> for a row. Leave out fields that do not apply.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>is_warmup</code></td>
          <td>boolean, defaults to false</td>
          <td>Marks a warmup set. Warmup sets do not count toward working volume.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>weight</code></td>
          <td>number</td>
          <td>
            A planned weight, in the unit named by <code>weight_unit</code>. Most shared programs
            leave it out so each lifter's own weights fill in.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>weight_unit</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td><code>"kg"</code> or <code>"lbs"</code>. Weights are stored in the unit they were entered in, never converted. Absent means kg.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>reps</code></td>
          <td>integer</td>
          <td>The rep target. With <code>reps_max</code> present it is the bottom of a range instead.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>reps_max</code></td>
          <td>integer</td>
          <td>
            Top of a rep range: <code>reps: 8, reps_max: 10</code> reads as 8-10. Leave it out for an
            exact target. Never valid on a warmup; import drops it there.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>is_amrap</code></td>
          <td>boolean, defaults to false</td>
          <td>
            An open-topped target: <code>reps</code> becomes a required minimum with no ceiling,
            shown as <code>8+</code>. <code>reps: 1, is_amrap: true</code> is how a set to failure is
            written. Mutually exclusive with <code>reps_max</code>: if a set carries both, import
            keeps the open target and drops the range. Never valid on a warmup.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><code>duration_seconds</code></td><td>integer</td><td>Planned time, for timed exercises.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>distance</code></td><td>number</td><td>Planned distance in kilometers.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>percent_of_reference</code></td>
          <td>number</td>
          <td>
            This set's weight as a percentage of the slot's
            <a href="#clause-terms">reference weight</a>: <code>85.0</code> is 85%. Badger works out
            the load from the recipient's own reference weight and snaps it to a weight they can
            load. Leave it out for an ordinary set with an absolute weight. Import accepts values
            above 0 and up to 200; anything outside that is read as an ordinary absolute-weight set.
            <br /><br />
            <strong><code>weight</code> is optional beside it.</strong> While the percentage applies,
            Badger ignores the weight but keeps it, so turning the percentage off restores the
            number. The built-in program files leave the weight out. Needs Badger 1.1.12 or later.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>is_progression_driver</code></td>
          <td>boolean, defaults to false</td>
          <td>
            Marks the <a href="#clause-terms">driver set</a>: the set whose logged reps the
            <code>driver_reps_at_least</code>, <code>driver_reps_between</code>,
            <code>driver_reps_below_plan</code> and <code>cycle_missed_minimum</code> triggers read.
            <strong>At most one per week of the slot's cycle, and never on a warmup.</strong> On a
            <a href="#waves">waved</a> day each <code>week_index</code> may have its own driver, as
            5/3/1's plus set does in weeks 1 to 3. A set with no <code>week_index</code> belongs to
            every week, so it rules out any other driver in the slot. If a file carries more than
            that, import keeps the first in array order and clears the rest, and a driver on a
            warmup is dropped. Needs Badger 1.1.12 or later.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>week_index</code></td>
          <td>integer</td>
          <td>
            Which week of its day's <a href="#waves">cycle</a> this set belongs to, counting from
            <strong>0</strong>. Leave it out and the set applies to <em>every</em> week: that is how
            every set on an unwaved day is written, and how a 5/3/1 file writes its accessory work
            once instead of four times. A value at or above the day's <code>cycle_length</code> is
            out of range, and the set applies to every week.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>target_rpe</code></td>
          <td>integer</td>
          <td>
            How hard the set should feel, on the RPE scale: <code>8</code> is RPE 8, about two reps
            left in reserve. Whole numbers from 6 to 10. Import rounds a half up (<code>8.5</code>
            becomes 9) and moves a value outside 6 to 10 to the nearer end. Mutually exclusive with
            <code>target_rir</code>: if a set carries both, import keeps <code>target_rpe</code> and
            drops <code>target_rir</code>. Never valid on a warmup; import drops it there. It is shown
            on the set as a target, and no progression trigger reads it. Needs Badger 1.1.12 or
            later.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>target_rir</code></td>
          <td>integer</td>
          <td>
            The same target in reps in reserve: <code>2</code> means stop with two reps left. Whole
            numbers from 0 to 5. Import drops any fraction (<code>2.5</code> becomes 2) and moves a
            value outside 0 to 5 to the nearer end. Write <code>target_rpe</code> or
            <code>target_rir</code>, never both. Never valid on a warmup; import drops it there.
            Needs Badger 1.1.12 or later.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="waves">Waves: cycle_length and week_index</h3>

  <p>
    A day whose sets change from week to week is written once, not once per week. 5/3/1's four-week
    cycle is the standard case. Two keys do it, and they only make sense together. Both need Badger
    1.1.12 or later.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="#day"><code>Day</code></a>'s <code>cycle_length</code> says how many weeks the cycle
      runs for. Absent means the day is not waved.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each <a href="#predefined-set"><code>PredefinedSet</code></a>'s <code>week_index</code> says
      which week that set belongs to, counting from 0. Absent means <strong>every week</strong>,
      which is how accessory work done all cycle long is written once.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A <code>week_index</code> that matches no week (negative, or at or above the day's
    <code>cycle_length</code>) is read as every week. The recipient starts the program at its first
    week: which week the sender is on does not travel.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The week counter belongs to the routine, not the day.</strong> It is one number that
    every waved day in the routine reads. It moves on once every day waved to the same length has
    been trained since the last move. Stage-based days (below) do not move it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each day reads the counter against its own <code>cycle_length</code>, so give every waved day in
    a routine the same length. <code>cycle_complete</code> fires when the counter wraps back to the
    first week. If lengths differ, each length moves the shared counter on its own schedule, and
    <code>cycle_complete</code> wraps at each day's own length.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Stage-based programs</strong> (GZCLP, StrongLifts) use the same two keys, with each
    <code>week_index</code> a stage and <code>cycle_length</code> the number of stages.
  </p>

  <p>
    A slot whose
    rule has a <code>next_stage</code> or <code>reset_stage</code> clause, or any clause targeting
    <code>stage</code>, shows the variant for its current stage. The stage changes only when one of
    those clauses fires; in GZCLP and StrongLifts that happens when you stall. Every other slot shows
    the variant for the routine's current week.
  </p>

  <h2 id="example">A complete routine file</h2>

  <p>
    One routine, one day scheduled Monday and Thursday, two exercises. The bench press has a warmup
    set and three working sets of 8-10; the plank is timed. This is the real
    <a href="/programs/example-routine.badger" download>example-routine.badger</a>, indented for
    reading. Files the app writes are minified, and whitespace makes no difference either way.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{examplePretty}</code></pre>

  <p>
    For files that use progression rules, percentage sets and waves, open the files on the
    <a href="/programs/">Programs</a> page, such as
    <a href="/programs/531-bbb.badger" download>531-bbb.badger</a> and
    <a href="/programs/gzclp.badger" download>gzclp.badger</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="progression-rules">Progression rules</h2>

  <p>
    A slot's progression rule says what happens to its weight after each session. It is a
    <a href="#progression"><code>Progression</code></a> object holding a list of
    <a href="#clause"><code>Clause</code></a> objects. <a href="#progression-example">The example
    below</a> shows a complete one.
  </p>

  <h3 id="progression">Progression</h3>

  <p>
    The object in a <a href="#day-exercise"><code>DayExercise</code></a>'s <code>progression</code>
    key. At most one per slot. Needs Badger 1.1.12 or later.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The rule travels; the sender's progress against it does not</strong>, including their
    training max (see <a href="#not-carried">what a file does not carry</a>). A
    <code>training_max</code> key written into a file is an unknown key and is ignored.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>cadence</code></td>
          <td>string, required</td>
          <td>
            How often the program means the rule to be looked at: <code>session</code>,
            <code>week</code> or <code>cycle</code>. Badger stores it and nothing reads it, like
            <code>scheme</code> below. Badger evaluates the most recent qualifying session whenever
            the log preview opens. An unrecognized value still drops the whole rule.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>reference_kind</code></td>
          <td>string, required</td>
          <td>
            Which number the rule moves: <code>working_weight</code> (a real weight on the bar) or
            <code>training_max</code>. A <code>working_weight</code> rule may not carry a clause
            targeting <code>reference</code>, and a file that has one loses the rule.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>clauses</code></td>
          <td>array of <a href="#clause"><code>Clause</code></a>, required</td>
          <td>
            The rule itself, <strong>in evaluation order</strong>: the first clause whose trigger
            matches is the one that fires. Any clauses directly after it that state the same
            <code>trigger</code> with the same numbers, and move a different <code>target</code>,
            fire with it; that is how one outcome both resets the stage and cuts the weight. Nothing
            else runs. Must be non-empty and must end with an <code>always</code> clause.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>scheme</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            Where the rule came from, as a label: <code>linear</code>,
            <code>double_progression</code>, <code>gzclp_t1</code>, <code>gzclp_t2</code>,
            <code>gzclp_t3</code>, <code>wave_531</code>, <code>nsuns_t1</code>,
            <code>nsuns_t2</code>, <code>madcow_weekly</code>, <code>custom</code>. Nothing behaves
            differently because of it; the clauses are the behavior. An unrecognized value reads as
            <code>custom</code>, and the rule still imports.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>note</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            The author's own line about the rule. Displayed word for word and never parsed, like an
            <a href="#accessory">accessory's</a> <code>label</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="clause">Clause</h3>

  <p>
    The objects in <code>clauses</code>. Each one is a trigger and the action it fires.
    <code>count</code>, <code>reps_min</code> and <code>reps_max</code> belong to the trigger;
    <code>target</code>, <code>amount</code>, <code>amount_unit</code> and <code>percent</code>
    belong to the action. The terms missed session, failure counter, stage and remembered weight are
    defined under <a href="#clause-terms">Terms used below</a>, at the start of the specification.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>trigger</code></td>
          <td>string, required</td>
          <td>
            What makes the clause fire. One of:
            <ul>
              <li><code>all_sets_met</code>: every planned working set reached its rep target (at or above the minimum, for a range).</li>
              <li><code>all_sets_top_of_range</code>: every ranged working set hit the top of its range. Open (<code>N+</code>) sets are skipped.</li>
              <li><code>any_set_missed</code>: at least one planned working set came in under its minimum.</li>
              <li><code>driver_reps_at_least</code>: the driver set's logged reps are at least <code>reps_min</code>.</li>
              <li><code>driver_reps_between</code>: the driver set's reps fall in <code>[reps_min, reps_max]</code>, inclusive at both ends.</li>
              <li><code>driver_reps_below_plan</code>: the driver set did not reach the minimum <em>its own plan row</em> asked for. It states no number: the minimum comes from the plan, and on a waved day that is this week's own minimum. Cannot fire when the session has no driver set, or when the driver's plan states no minimum.</li>
              <li><code>cycle_missed_minimum</code>: <code>driver_reps_below_plan</code> with a memory. A driver set has come up short at some point in the cycle now running, this session included. Clears when the cycle ends. A rule on a day with no <a href="#waves"><code>cycle_length</code></a> can never fire it.</li>
              <li><code>consecutive_failures_at_least</code>: the failure counter has reached <code>count</code>, counting this session.</li>
              <li><code>stage_is_last</code>: the slot is on its final stage and the session failed.</li>
              <li><code>cycle_complete</code>: the routine's cycle has come round since this slot last acted.</li>
              <li><code>always</code>: unconditionally. The terminal "otherwise" clause every rule ends with.</li>
            </ul>
            Any other value drops the whole rule.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>count</code></td>
          <td>integer</td>
          <td>
            How many qualifying sessions in a row the trigger needs. Leave it out and the trigger
            fires on a single occurrence. A written <code>1</code> behaves the same as an absent key,
            but Badger prints the written one as "for 1 session".
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>reps_min</code> / <code>reps_max</code></td>
          <td>integer</td>
          <td>
            Inclusive rep bounds for <code>driver_reps_at_least</code> and
            <code>driver_reps_between</code>. With both left out, the trigger does not look at reps
            at all; they are never defaulted to zero or to the plan's own numbers. Either of those
            two triggers with no <code>reps_min</code>, or a <code>reps_max</code> below the
            <code>reps_min</code>, drops the whole rule.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>action</code></td>
          <td>string, required</td>
          <td>
            What happens when it fires. One of:
            <ul>
              <li><code>add_absolute</code>: add <code>amount</code>.</li>
              <li><code>add_percent</code>: change the weight by <code>percent</code> percent. <code>5</code> adds 5%, <code>-10</code> takes 10% off.</li>
              <li><code>hold</code>: the weight stays; counters still move.</li>
              <li><code>none</code>: nothing changes at all, counters included.</li>
              <li><code>next_stage</code>: move up a stage, weight unchanged.</li>
              <li><code>reset_stage</code>: back to the first stage (stage 0), weight unchanged. To reset and change the weight on the same outcome, follow it with a second clause carrying the same <code>trigger</code> and a weight action, as GZCLP does: <code>reset_stage</code> on <code>stage</code>, then <code>add_percent</code> <code>-10</code> on <code>working_weight</code>.</li>
              <li><code>set_from_remembered</code>: the remembered weight plus <code>amount</code>. With nothing remembered, the clause does nothing and says so.</li>
              <li><code>halve_increment</code>: halve the increment this rule adds from here on.</li>
            </ul>
            Any other value drops the whole rule.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>target</code></td>
          <td>string, required</td>
          <td>
            What the action moves: <code>working_weight</code>, <code>reference</code> (the training
            max), <code>stage</code>, or <code>increment</code>. Required on every clause, including
            <code>hold</code> and <code>none</code>. The pairs Badger's own programs use:
            <ul>
              <li><code>add_absolute</code> (needs <code>amount</code>), <code>add_percent</code> (needs <code>percent</code>), <code>set_from_remembered</code> (needs <code>amount</code>), <code>hold</code> and <code>none</code>: the weight the rule moves, which is <code>reference</code> in a <code>training_max</code> rule and <code>working_weight</code> otherwise.</li>
              <li><code>next_stage</code> and <code>reset_stage</code>: <code>stage</code>.</li>
              <li><code>halve_increment</code>: <code>increment</code>, with <code>amount</code> and <code>amount_unit</code> set to the rule's <code>add_absolute</code> step, which is what it halves the first time.</li>
            </ul>
            The only pairing that drops a rule is <code>reference</code> in a
            <code>working_weight</code> rule.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>amount</code></td>
          <td>number</td>
          <td>
            The absolute amount, in the unit named by <code>amount_unit</code>. Required for
            <code>add_absolute</code> and <code>set_from_remembered</code>; leaving it out on either
            drops the whole rule. It never falls back to the exercise's own increment, so write the
            number.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>amount_unit</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td><code>"kg"</code> or <code>"lbs"</code>, for <code>amount</code>. Absent means kg.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>percent</code></td>
          <td>number</td>
          <td>
            The percentage for <code>add_percent</code>: <code>-10</code> means drop 10%. Required
            for that action and meaningless for any other.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="caps">What drops a rule</span>
    <p>
      If a rule breaks any of these, import skips the <em>whole</em> rule and counts it in the
      import preview's warning. The rest of the file imports normally.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>An unrecognized <code>trigger</code>, <code>action</code>, <code>target</code>, <code>cadence</code> or <code>reference_kind</code>.</li>
      <li>A <code>driver_reps_at_least</code> or <code>driver_reps_between</code> trigger with no <code>reps_min</code>. (<code>driver_reps_below_plan</code> takes its minimum from the plan.)</li>
      <li>A <code>driver_reps_between</code> whose <code>reps_max</code> is below its <code>reps_min</code>.</li>
      <li>A missing <code>amount</code> or <code>percent</code> that the action requires.</li>
      <li>A clause targeting <code>reference</code> under <code>reference_kind: "working_weight"</code>.</li>
      <li>No clauses at all, or a <code>clauses</code> array that does not <em>end</em> with an <code>always</code>. An <code>always</code> in the middle leaves every clause after it unable to fire.</li>
      <li>A clause that can never fire because an earlier one already covers it, such as <code>consecutive_failures_at_least</code> 2 before the same trigger at 3, or a run of same-trigger clauses that moves one target twice.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h3 id="progression-example">Example: a linear rule</h3>

  <p>
    Add 2.5 kg after every session in which all working sets hit their target, take 10% off after
    three missed sessions in a row, and otherwise keep the weight. This is one slot, with three sets
    of 5:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{ruleExamplePretty}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Badger reads the clauses in order and fires the first that matches. After a third missed session
    in a row, the first clause takes 10% off and resets the failure counter to 0. After a session
    where every planned working set reached 5 reps, the second clause adds 2.5 kg. Any other session
    reaches the <code>always</code> clause, and the weight holds while the failure counter still
    moves.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rule ends with <code>always</code>, every clause names a <code>target</code>, and
    <code>add_absolute</code> and <code>add_percent</code> carry the <code>amount</code> and
    <code>percent</code> they need, so none of the conditions above drops it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gym">Gym and machines payloads</h2>

  <p>
    <code>type: "gym"</code> shares a whole gym: the gym itself, its machines, attachments, bars and
    accessories, which of each belong to it, its dumbbell and kettlebell racks, and its plates.
    <code>type: "machines"</code> is the smaller version: one key, <code>machines</code>, an array
    of <a href="#machine"><code>Machine</code></a>, with no gym, bars, accessories, or inventory.
  </p>

  <p>
    A gym file carries what describes the gym. Notes about a machine travel. Your photo of it, and
    the setup cues you saved against one exercise on it, stay on your phone.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>gym</code></td><td><code>Gym</code>, required</td><td>A <code>Gym</code> is just <code>&#123; "name": "..." &#125;</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>machines</code></td><td>array of <a href="#machine"><code>Machine</code></a></td><td>The same <code>Machine</code> objects a routine payload carries.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>attachments</code></td><td>array of <code>Attachment</code></td><td>An <code>Attachment</code> is a <code>name</code> and optional <code>notes</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>bars</code></td><td>array of <a href="#bar"><code>Bar</code></a></td><td>Named bars. Omitted when the gym has none.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>accessories</code></td><td>array of <a href="#accessory"><code>Accessory</code></a></td><td>Bands, chains, and straps. Omitted when the gym has none.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gym_machines</code></td><td>array of strings</td><td>Names of the machines that belong to this gym.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gym_attachments</code></td><td>array of strings</td><td>Names of the attachments that belong to this gym.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gym_bars</code></td><td>array of strings</td><td>Names of the bars that belong to this gym.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gym_accessories</code></td><td>array of strings</td><td>Names of the accessories that belong to this gym.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>racks</code></td><td>array of <a href="#rack"><code>Rack</code></a></td><td>The gym's dumbbell and kettlebell racks. Omitted when the gym has none set up.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>plate_inventory</code></td><td>array of <a href="#plate-inventory"><code>PlateInventory</code></a></td><td>The plates the gym actually has. Omitted when nobody has set them up.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Equipment is defined in its own arrays, and the four <code>gym_*</code> lists name which of it
    belongs to this gym, so one machine can belong to several gyms. Files Badger writes list exactly
    the objects defined beside them.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Leave a membership list out to include everything
    defined in the file.</strong> Writing it as <code>[]</code> means none of them, which leaves the
    gym with nothing of that kind and is almost never what you want.
  </p>

  <h3 id="machine">Machine</h3>

  <p>
    The objects in <code>machines</code>, in a routine payload and a gym or machines payload alike.
    In a routine payload, importing the machines is a toggle the recipient controls. In a gym or
    machines payload, machines are always created.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>string, required</td><td>Machine name.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>notes</code></td><td>string</td><td>Free text about the machine, such as "third from the left, seat pin sticks".</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>uses_attachments</code></td><td>boolean, defaults to false</td><td>Whether the machine takes attachments (bars, handles, ropes).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>uses_grip_types</code></td><td>boolean, defaults to false</td><td>Whether grip is worth recording on this machine.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>supports_bands</code></td>
          <td>boolean, defaults to false</td>
          <td>
            Whether bands can be attached here. It decides whether a machine exercise offers
            <a href="#accessory">accessories</a> at all, and then only band-kind ones.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>machine_type</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td><code>"selectorized"</code> (a stack with a pin) or <code>"plate_loaded"</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>weight_increment</code></td>
          <td>number</td>
          <td>Smallest real step on this machine, in the machine's own unit.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>weight_increment2</code></td>
          <td>number</td>
          <td>Second step size, for a stack whose plates get heavier further up.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>increment_threshold</code></td>
          <td>number</td>
          <td>Weight at and above which <code>weight_increment2</code> applies.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>plate_calculator_mode</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            <code>"dual_loaded"</code> (plates on both sides) or <code>"single_loaded"</code> (one
            lever arm).
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>bar_weight</code></td>
          <td>number</td>
          <td>
            Weight of the bar or empty carriage, in the unit named by <code>bar_weight_unit</code>,
            so a 45 lb bar travels as <code>45</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>bar_weight_unit</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td><code>"kg"</code> or <code>"lbs"</code>, for <code>bar_weight</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>weight_unit</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td><code>"kg"</code> or <code>"lbs"</code>, when this machine is labeled in a different unit from the rest of your logging. Absent means the machine follows the recipient's own logging unit, and <code>weight_increment</code>, <code>weight_increment2</code> and <code>increment_threshold</code> are read in that unit. Write it whenever the file states those numbers.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>logs_weight_per_side</code></td>
          <td>boolean</td>
          <td>
            What a weight logged on this machine means: <code>true</code> for one side (iso-lateral
            machines), <code>false</code> for the total. Leave the key out if the machine has no
            preference; Badger then uses the exercise's own setting, which is not the same as
            writing <code>false</code>. Only meaningful on a plate-loaded machine; a value on any
            other kind is ignored.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="bar">Bar</h3>

  <p>The objects in <code>bars</code>: a named bar, with its own weight.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>string, required</td><td>Bar name, for example "Olympic bar" or "Trap bar".</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>notes</code></td><td>string</td><td>Free text about the bar.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>bar_weight</code></td>
          <td>number</td>
          <td>
            What the bar weighs empty, in the unit named by <code>bar_weight_unit</code>, so a 45 lb
            bar travels as <code>45</code>. Leave it out for a bar nobody has weighed; Badger then
            falls back to the machine's or the exercise's own bar weight.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>bar_weight_unit</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td><code>"kg"</code> or <code>"lbs"</code>, for <code>bar_weight</code>. Absent means kg.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="accessory">Accessory</h3>

  <p>The objects in <code>accessories</code>: a band, a chain, a set of suspension straps, or another add-on.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>string, required</td><td>Accessory name, for example "Green band".</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>kind</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            One of <code>band</code>, <code>chain</code>, <code>suspension</code>, <code>other</code>.
            A missing or unrecognized value reads as <code>other</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>color</code></td>
          <td>integer</td>
          <td>
            Packed ARGB, for example <code>4283215696</code>. Omitted when the accessory has no color
            set. It travels because people name a band by its color.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>label</code></td>
          <td>string</td>
          <td>
            Free text shown beside the name, for example <code>"rated 25 lbs"</code>. Carried word
            for word and never parsed. Accessories never feed any calculation in Badger, so a number
            in here stays text.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><code>notes</code></td><td>string</td><td>Free text about the accessory.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="rack">Rack</h3>

  <p>
    The objects in <code>racks</code>: the dumbbells or kettlebells a gym owns. List every weight on
    the rack, since racks do not step evenly (5, 10, 15 up to 50, then a jump). The plus and minus
    buttons step through this list when you log.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>equipment</code></td><td>string, required</td><td><code>"dumbbell"</code> or <code>"kettlebell"</code>. Any other value is skipped.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>unit</code></td><td>string, required</td><td><code>"kg"</code> or <code>"lbs"</code>, for every weight in this rack.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>weights</code></td>
          <td>array of objects, required</td>
          <td>
            Each one is a <code>weight</code> (number, required) and an optional <code>color</code>
            (packed ARGB integer), for competition kettlebells, which are identified by color. Leave
            the color out for an unpainted weight.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    One entry per equipment-and-unit pair, so a gym with kilogram kettlebells and pound dumbbells
    carries two. <strong>Nothing is converted between them</strong>: 45 in a pound rack is a 45 lb
    dumbbell.
  </p>

  <h3 id="plate-inventory">PlateInventory</h3>

  <p>The objects in <code>plate_inventory</code>: what the plate calculator has to work with.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>unit</code></td><td>string, required</td><td><code>"kg"</code> or <code>"lbs"</code>, for every number in this entry.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>bar_weight</code></td><td>number</td><td>The bar the calculator starts from, in the unit above, so <code>45</code> in a pound entry is a 45 lb bar. Absent falls back to the ordinary bar for that unit.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>plates</code></td>
          <td>array of objects, required</td>
          <td>
            Each one is a <code>weight</code> (number, required), a <code>color</code> (packed ARGB
            integer), a <code>quantity</code> (how many are available <em>per side</em>, defaults to
            2) and <code>enabled</code> (defaults to true). A plate that exists but is switched off
            has <code>enabled: false</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    One entry per unit, and nothing converts between them. A gym still on Badger's built-in default
    plates writes no <code>plate_inventory</code> at all.
  </p>

  <h2 id="import-rules">What import does</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing is overwritten.</strong> Import only adds. An existing exercise, machine, gym,
      or routine is never edited by a file you open.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Exercises match on name and type</strong>, ignoring case and surrounding spaces. The
      same name with a different type is a different exercise. With no match, the exercise is
      created in the named category, which is created too if it is new.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Machines, attachments, bars, accessories, gyms, and categories match on name alone</strong>,
      again ignoring case and spaces.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A routine name collision does not merge.</strong> Importing a routine whose name you
      already use gives you "Name (imported)", then "Name (imported) 2", and so on. You keep both.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It is one transaction.</strong> A file that fails partway through leaves your database
      exactly as it was.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Days, slots, and sets import in array order.</strong></li>
    <li>
      <strong>A warmup never carries a rep range or an open target.</strong> Import drops
      <code>reps_max</code> and <code>is_amrap</code> from a warmup set. A working set carrying both
      keeps the open target and loses the range.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An unreadable progression rule is skipped whole</strong> and counted in the import
      preview's warning; the rest of the file imports normally. <a href="#clause">What drops a
      rule</a> is listed with the clause fields. Per-set flags are repaired instead: extra driver
      sets are cleared as described under
      <a href="#predefined-set"><code>is_progression_driver</code></a>, a driver on a warmup is
      cleared, and a <code>week_index</code> or <code>percent_of_reference</code> that cannot mean
      anything is read as absent. An intensity target is repaired as described under
      <a href="#predefined-set"><code>target_rpe</code></a>.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Files over 10 MB are refused</strong> without being read.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="not-carried">What a file does not carry</h2>

  <p>A routine file is the plan, and only the parts of it that work for a different lifter in a different gym.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>No history.</strong> No workouts, no logged sets, no PRs. To move your own history to a new phone, use a backup instead: see <a href="/help/phone-migration/">moving to a new phone</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>No notes below the routine level.</strong> Routine notes travel; per-day and per-exercise notes do not. In a gym file, notes on a machine, attachment, bar or accessory do travel. The setup cues you save against one exercise on one machine never do.</li>
    <li><strong>No default attachment, default bar, or default grip</strong> on a slot, and no superset group <strong>color</strong>; the recipient's palette assigns it. (An <a href="#accessory">accessory's</a> color does travel.)</li>
    <li>
      <strong>No training max, and no progress against a rule.</strong> A slot's
      <a href="#progression">progression rule</a> travels. The training max it multiplies does not,
      and neither does any counter behind it: a run of missed sessions, a stage, a cycle position,
      which session was last evaluated. The recipient sets their own training max, from their own
      history, the first time they run the program.
    </li>
    <li><strong>No per-exercise detail beyond name, type, and category.</strong> An exercise that gets created on import starts blank: no instructions, no muscles, no rest default, no increment. Anything you already had keeps everything it already has.</li>
    <li><strong>No photos</strong> of machines, attachments, or bars.</li>
    <li><strong>Nothing about you.</strong> No account, no device identifier, no settings, no training notes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="versioning">Versions and compatibility</h2>

  <p>
    Unknown fields are ignored, so a file written by a newer Badger still opens in an older one,
    minus whatever that older version has no concept of. If the shape ever has to change in a way
    that breaks, <code>badger</code> gets a new value and <code>share/v1</code> keeps being read.
  </p>

  <p>
    A file you exported today should still import years from now. See also
    <a href="/promise/">the promise</a>.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Optional keys are left out when unused.</strong> A file that uses none of the newer
      keys is byte-identical to one written before they existed, and an older copy of Badger reads
      it unchanged.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>1.1.12 keys.</strong> <code>progression</code>, <code>progression_group_key</code>,
      <code>percent_reference</code>, <code>percent_of_reference</code>,
      <code>is_progression_driver</code>, <code>cycle_length</code>, <code>week_index</code>,
      <code>target_rpe</code> and <code>target_rir</code> need Badger 1.1.12 or later. An older
      version ignores <code>progression</code> and imports the slot without a rule. When a
      percentage set also carries a <code>weight</code>, an older version that has never heard of
      <code>percent_of_reference</code> still finds the weight as the target. It reads a set with an
      intensity target as the same set with no target.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>is_amrap</code></strong> is omitted when false. Because <code>reps</code> still
      holds the minimum, an older version reads an open target as an exact one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>logs_weight_per_side</code></strong>: older versions ignore the key and lose
      nothing else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Older values still read.</strong> <code>machine_type: "plateLoaded"</code>; the
      camelCase <code>plate_calculator_mode</code> values; a machine's <code>bar_weight_kg</code>
      (always kg) in place of <code>bar_weight</code> and <code>bar_weight_unit</code>; and the
      retired <code>sort_order</code> field, which is ignored.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="caps">Verified</span>
    <p>
      Last checked against Badger <strong>{appVersion}</strong>, schema <code>share/v1</code>. The
      example above and the files on the <a href="/programs/">Programs</a> page were exported by that
      build and imported back into it before publishing. The <a href="#progression">progression</a>,
      percentage, driver and wave keys were checked against Badger <strong>1.1.12</strong>'s own
      import and export code. The published files are regenerated whenever the seeded programs
      change.
    </p>
  </div>
